   Wolverhampton, Wolverhampton, ENGLAND, MAY 09-12, 1999},
Organization = {Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Appl Sci; Univ Wolverhampton, Sch Engn; Univ
   Wolverhampton, Built Environm},
Abstract = {The deleterious effects of the dry deposition of sulphur dioxide (SO2)
   on carbonate stone monuments and buildings are of grave concern in the
   realm Of materials conservation. The resulting formation of gypsum
   crusts often leads to catastrophic loss of material. While much
   attention has been given to this problem, identifying and understanding
   the mechanisms underlying this deterioration process is extremely
   difficult due to the vast number of variables involved in an outdoor
   environment. Additionally, these mechanisms often act in tandem with
   natural weathering processes. This paper briefly discusses or cites the
   fundamental laboratory studies on this subject in order to present an
   overview of the trends of research in this area and to contribute
   towards indicating directions of future study.},
