   (SC), Alicante, SPAIN, 2016},
Organization = {Wessex Inst; Univ Alicante; WIT Transact Ecol \& Environm; Int Journal
   Sustainable Dev \& Planning; Int Journal Design \& Nat \& Ecodynam},
Abstract = {Pavement distress prediction models (PDPM) are normally sought as one of
   the tools in pavement management systems (PMS). These tools attempt in
   line with modelling method deployed to capture the progressive nature of
   pavement surfacing layers' distress as dependent parameter versus
   causative parameters which serve as independent parameters.
   The process of formulating PDPM faces a number of challenges which
   affects its accuracy in prediction of future state of distresses in
   question, and hence its effectiveness as a tool for managing road
   pavements. The list of challenges to overcome is long, but can be
   categorised in terms of behaviour and characteristics of independent and
   dependent variables.
   Based on the study carried out in Iringa region, Tanzania, the paper
   looks at challenges noted in an attempt to answer questions such as
   which variables stand out as most appropriate in reflecting the dynamic
   nature of pavement distresses so as to serve as dependent or independent
   variables. Answers provided may serve to improve the formulating process
   and reliability of PDPM.},
