   Antalya, TURKEY, MAY 25-29, 2011},
Abstract = {The purpose of this study is to understand how playing digital games
   affect children and adolescents. relationships with their parents.
   Ninety primary and secondary school students from 6 schools were
   randomly invited to participate in a one-on-one qualitative
   semi-structured interview. Mixed responses were received from the
   students, with regard to whether gaming displaced time with their family
   and whether it caused conflicts with parents. On a positive note, the
   majority of them felt that playing games did not displace time with
   parents or lead to conflicts with parents. This paper presents a
   detailed account of their perception and reasons on the issue. (C) 2011
   Published by Elsevier Ltd. Selection and/or peer-review under
